Abstract
Herein, novel photoactive materials, MOF-derived porous hollow carbon nanobubbles $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{nCdS}$ multi-shelled dodecahedral cages (C@ZnCdS MSDCs)， were synthesized via continuous chemical etching, sulfurization, cation-exchange and calcination strategies. Due to the synergistic effect between the porous shells and the carbon-layer coating， C@ZnCdS MSDCs displayed superior photoelectrochemical (PEC) performance. The synthesized C@ZnCdS MSDCs were assembled onto $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ modified ITO electrodes to form a type-II heterostructures. Then, Au nanoparticles (NPs) were deposited on the surface of ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs. Benefiting from the unique structure and performance merits of photoactive materials, a label-free PEC sensing platform based on ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au was successfully constructed for CEA detection. Under optimal conditions, the PEC biosensor exhibited a wide linear range $(0.00005\mathrm{~-~}500\mathrm{~ng~mL}^{-1}$ ) and low detection limit (2.28 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ). The proposed PEC biosensor also showed good stability, specificity, reproducibility and acceptability in human serum. The prepared $\mathrm{C@}\mathrm{ZnCdS}$ MSDCs would be a promising photoactive material for PEC biosensors. Most importantly, this work opens up new horizons for the application of MOFs-derived hollow carbon materials in sensing.
Keywords： C@ZnCdS multi-shelled dodecahedral cages; Photoactive material; Photoelectrochemical biosensor; CEA
## 1. Introduction
Photoelectrochemical (PEC) analysis ， as an emerging and promising technology, has gained increasing attention (Ge et al. 2018; Zhao et al. 2015). Thanks to its unparalleled merits of ultra-high sensitivity, low cost, rapid analysis, and easy of miniaturization, PEC biosensors have been used for the detection of various biomolecules, cells, and harmful gases, and so forth (Liu et al. 2018; Wei et al. 2018). Obviously, photoactive materials play a pivotal part in the performance of PEC biosensors. Hitherto, varieties of photoactive materials, such as ZnO (Feng et al. 2018), $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Wang et al. 2009), BiOI (Yan et al. 2015), CdS (Han et al. 2018), CdSe (Zhu et al. 2016), and ${\bf B i}_{2}{\bf S}_{3}$ (Yin et al. 2014), have been employed for the construction of photoelectrochemical biosensors. Nevertheless, some inherent defects impede their wide application, such as inefficient visible light absorption, tardigrade charge transfer, high recombination of photoinduced carriers, and so on (Wu et al. 2018). Therefore, the design and synthesis of innovative photoactive materials which have anticipant structure and sensing properties are still urgently needed.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with unique morphological characteristics have attracted extensive attention in recent years (García-García et al. 2014). Compared with the traditional porous materials, MOFs display some unique merits, including high specific surface area, controllable morphology, high porosity, and easily modification (Ling et al. 2016; Yang et al. 2018). In addition, porous carbon composites derived from MOF can inherit their corresponding structural properties. Moreover, MOF-derived porous carbon structure facilitates electron transport. Thus, they have been widely used in many fields, such as supercapacitors, gas storage, batteries and catalysts (Liu et al. 2016; Shen et al. 2016). It has been reported that MOFs and its derivatives can be used as photoactive materials to construct PEC sensors. For instance, Zhang et al. developed a PEC sensor based on zirconium-porphyrin MOFs for enzyme-free prostate specific antigen detection (Zhang et al. 2018). Lu et al. developed a PEC sensor based on MOF-derived porous $\mathrm{C}_{03}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ for volatile organic compounds detection (Lu et al. 2014). Zhang et al.
developed a PEC sensor based on Zirconium-porphyrin MOFs for label-free phosphoprotein detection (Zhang et al. 2016). Yang et al. developed a PEC sensor based on MOF-derived nitrogen-doped porous carbon-ZnO for label-free alkaline phosphatase detection (Yang et al. 2017). Many efforts have been committed to the application of MOFs and its derivatives to PEC sensing, and considerable progress has been made. However, the application of MOFs and its derivatives as photoactive materials is limited due to poor conductivity, high recombination of photo-induced carrier, and wide bandgap. Therefore, designing a reasonable synthesis strategy based on MOFs to prepare photoactive materials with good electrical conductivity, enhanced charge separation, and narrow bandgap remains a huge challenge.
Inspired by this, herein, MOF-derived porous hollow carbon nanobubbles $\ @\operatorname{ZnCdS}$ multi-shelled dodecahedral cages (C@ZnCdS MSDCs) were prepared by continuous chemical etching, sulfurization, cation-exchange and calcination strategies. To the best of our knowledge, C@ZnCdS MSDCs derived from MOF have not been reported elsewhere, neither has been applied to PEC sensors. The synthesized C@ZnCdS MSDCs exhibit the advantages of hollow nanostructures: (i) hollow nanostructures can facilitate light harvesting due to multiple scattering of light; (ii) the porous thin shells can shorten transport distance of charge, thereby improving the separation efficiency of $\mathrm{e^{-1}/h^{+}}$ (Wang et al. 2016; Xiao et al. 2018). It is well known that $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ possesses many advantages, such as high stability, low cost, large surface area, and good photoelectric activity, which are used as an excellent basic material for developing PEC biosensors (Li et al. 2012). However, the intrinsic drawbacks of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hinder its widespread application, such as low efficiency of visible light absorption and high recombination of photoinduced $\mathrm{e^{-1}/h^{+}}$ pairs (Liu et al. 2017). The synthesized narrow bandgap $\mathbf{C}@$ ZnCdS MSDCs $(\sim2.43\mathrm{eV})$ were assembled onto $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ modified ITO electrodes to form a type-II heterostructures. The photoexcited electrons were transferred from the conduction band (CB) of $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs to the CB of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Simultaneously, holes were transferred from the valence band (VB) of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to the VB of C@ZnCdS MSDCs, leading to boost of visible-light response and separation of photogenerated $\mathrm{e^{-1}/h^{+}}$ . Afterward, electrodeposition of Au NPs on ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs surface can further ameliorate PEC performance due to its high electron conductivity. Furthermore, the decorated Au NPs can act as an effective electron acceptor for C@ZnCdS MSDCs, suppressing charge recombination. Based on the outstanding PEC performance of ITO/TiO2/C $@$ ZnCdS MSDCs/Au, a label-free sensing platform was successfully constructed. Here, taking CEA as a model because of its pivotal part in the clinical diagnosis and monitoring of many cancers (Hasanzadeh et al. 2017). The PEC biosensor exhibited a wide linear range $(0.00005-500~\mathrm{ng~mL}^{-1}.$ ) with low LOD of $2.28~\mathrm{fg~mL^{-1}}$ , indicating that MOF-derived C@ZnCdS MSDCs would be a promising photoactive material for PEC biosensors.
## 2. Experimental Section
Material and reagents, and apparatus have been reported on supporting information.
## 2.1. Syntheses of ZIF-8 dodecahedral cages and ZIF-8 yolk-shelled dodecahedral cages
ZIF-8 dodecahedral cages (DCs) were synthesized according to the previous literature with minor modification (Dong et al. 2017). $2.36\mathrm{~g~}$ of Zinc nitrate hexahydrate and $\ensuremath{1.32\mathrm{~g~}}$ of 2-methylimidazole were separately dissolved in methanol ( $100~\mathrm{{mL})}$ . Then $\underline{{1.32~\mathrm{g}}}$ of 2-methylimidazole solution was quickly added into the $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ solution under stirring. After 1 minute, the mixed solution was incubated at room temperature for $11\mathrm{~h~}$ . The obtained ZIF-8 DCs were centrifuged, washed with ethanol 3 times, and dried under vacuum at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 hours. ZIF-8 yolk-shelled dodecahedral cages (YSDCs) were prepared by adding $40~\mathrm{\mg}$ of as-prepared ZIF-8 DCs to $200~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol solution containing $\mathrm{~1~g~}$ of tannic acid (TA). After stirring $30~\mathrm{min}$ , ZIF-8 YSDCs were collected by centrifugation, washed with ethanol 3 times, and dried under vacuum at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 hours.
## 2.2. Syntheses of porous hollow ZnS double-shelled dodecahedral cages
$20~\mathrm{mg}$ of the as-prepared ZIF-8 YSDCs were placed into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol solution containing $400\ \mathrm{mg}$ of thioacetamide (TAA). After stirring $3\mathrm{min}$ , the solution was poured into $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ autoclave and heated for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\$ Afterward, porous hollow $Z\mathrm{nS}$ double-shelled dodecahedral cages ( $\mathrm{\ZnS}$ DSDCs) were collected by centrifugation, washed with ethanol 3 times, and dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 hours.
2.3. Syntheses of porous hollow nanobubbles ZnCdS double-shelled dodecahedral cages and C@ZnCdS MSDCs
## 2.4. Fabrication of ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au electrodes
Before modification, the ITO electrodes were successively treated by acetone, 1.0 M NaOH solution, ultrapure water. Firstly, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $5.0\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}\ T i O_{2}}$ aqueous suspension was introduced onto the pre-treated ITO electrode and dried naturally. Subsequently, $6~\mathrm{\textmuL}$ of $5.0~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ ZnCdS DSDCs aqueous suspension was dropped onto the as-prepared $\mathrm{ITO/TiO}_{2}$ electrode. After drying, the above electrode was sintered at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under air for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs electrode was obtained. Afterward, electrodeposition of Au NPs on $\mathrm{ITO/TiO_{2}/C@Z n C d S}$ MSDCs surface was carried out by cyclic voltammetric (CV) scanning from $-0.2\mathrm{V}$ to $0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ in $0.5\mathrm{\mM\HAuCl_{4}}$ at a scan rate of $50~\mathrm{mV/s}$ for 10 cycles. After rinsing, the ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au electrodes were fabricated successfully. Finally, for comparison, the $\mathrm{ITO/TiO}_{2}$ , ITO/ZnCdS MSDCs, ITO/C@ZnCdS MSDCs, and ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs electrodes were prepared in the same manner.
## 2.5. Construction of the PEC biosensor
Firstly, $8~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of anti-CEA $(30\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}},$ ) was dropped on the above obtained ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au electrode surface at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ , and then rinsed with PBS. Secondly, the obtained $\Pi{\mathrm{O/TiO}}_{2}/\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{ZnCdS}$ MSDCs/Au/anti-CEA electrode was covered with $6~\upmu\mathrm{L}~1$ wt $\%$ bovine serum albumin in $0.1\textbf{M}$ PBS (pH 7.4) for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ to block non-specific adsorption, followed by washing with PBS. Finally, $8~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ CEA solutions with different concentrations were dropped onto the electrodes respectively and incubated at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . After rinsing, the PEC biosensor (Scheme 1B) was successfully fabricated and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ until use.
## 2.6. PEC measurements
The PEC measurements were performed using a photoelectrochemical workstation in $0.1\textbf{M}$ PBS $\mathrm{(pH~}7.4\mathrm{)}$ solution containing $0.2{\bf\delta M}$ ascorbic acid (AA). The applied potential was $0\mathrm{~V~}$ with a $430~\mathrm{nm}$ light as excitation source and a light intensity of $180~\mathrm{W~m}^{-2}$ . A three-electrode system was used with a saturated $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ electrode as the reference electrode, a Pt wire as the counter electrode, and the as-prepared TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au modified ITO electrode $(3.0{\times}1.0~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ as the working electrode.
