## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of C@ZnCdS MSDCs
The synthesis procedure of C@ZnCdS MSDCs is schematically depicted in Scheme 1A. First, ZIF-8 DCs are synthesized by self-assembly of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and 2-methylimmidazole. The obtained ZIF-8 DCs are then converted to ZIF-8 YSDCs by a polymer acid etching. In this process, TA which acts as etchant and protector releases protons to penetrate the surface and reach the central region of the ZIF-8 DCs (Zhang et al. 2017b). Simultaneously, it is uniformly adsorbed on the surfaces of the ZIF-8 DCs due to planar conformation and abundance of hydroxyl group, thus protecting the outer region of the ZIF-8 DCs (Hu et al. 2016; Zhang et al. 2017a). Subsequently, $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions located on the surface of the ZIF-8 DCs react with $S^{2-}$ ions released from TAA in the solvothermal treatment, ultimately, forming ZnS DSDCs. To prepare the $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs, a metal cation-exchange reaction between $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ is performed. Since the solubility product constant $(K_{\mathrm{sp}})$ of $Z\mathrm{nS}$ $(1.6\times10^{-24})$ is larger than CdS $(8.0\times10^{-27},$ ), the structural conversion of $Z\mathrm{nS}$ DSDCs to $Z_{\mathrm{{nCdS}}}$ DSDCs is induced by ion exchange (Chen et al. 2017; Choi et al. 2016). Afterwards, the C@ZnCdS MSDCs are fabricated by the carbonization of ZnCdS DSDCs.
The microstructure and morphology of as-prepared samples are obtained by FESEM analysis, as shown in Figure 1. The ZIF-8 DCs (Figure 1A) and ZIF-8 YSDCs (Figure 1B) exhibit a typical rhombic dodecahedron with an average particle size of about $800~\mathrm{nm}$ . Besides, the ZIF-8 YSDCs have the same crystal structure as ZIF-8 DCs (Figure S1). It is noteworthy that the ZnS DSDCs (Figure 1C) have a unique double-shelled structure. Then, the obtained ZnS DSDCs are converted into double-shelled ZnCdS DCs (Figure 1D) through a solvothermal cation-exchange reaction at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ The triple-shelled C@ZnCdS DCs are obtained after calcining the double-shelled ZnCdS DCs precursor in Muffle furnace, with similar rhombic dodecahedron structure and size being maintained. Besides, the obtained $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs have a pore diameter distribution in the range of $25{-}40\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1E).
TEM image (Figure 2A, B) clearly exhibits the unique triple-shelled hollow structure. The microstructure of C@ZnCdS MSDCs is further elucidated by high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) lattice image (Figure 2C) and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Figure 2D). The marked lattice spacing of $0.33~\mathrm{nm}$ and 0.19 nm corresponds to that of (002) and (110) plane of CdS (JCPDS, No. 77-2306) and ZnS (JCPDS, No. 75-1547), respectively. The SAED pattern (Figure 2D) obtained from a single cage exhibits bright diffraction rings, indicating the cage is polycrystalline (Jiang et al. 2012). In addition, high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) further reveals a carbon-layer coating on the surface of material (Figure 2E). Elemental mapping images indicate the uniform distribution of Zn, Cd, S and C elements in C@ZnCdS MSDCs. Thus, through the sequential chemical etching, sulfurization and calcination strategy, porous hollow carbon nanobubbles@ZnCdS triple-shelled dodecahedral cages are obtained.
PXRD pattern is utilized to further investigate the crystal structure of $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs. In Figure 3A, the characteristic peaks at 24.8 °, 26.5 °, 28.2 °, 36.7 °, 43.7 °,47.9 °, and 51.9 °observed in PXRD patterns match well with the standard CdS (JCPDS, No. 77-2306), the peaks at $26.8~^{\circ},28.4~^{\circ},47.4~^{\circ}$ , and $51.6^{\circ}$ can be assigned to ZnS (JCPDS, No. 75-1547). The Raman spectra of $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs are displayed in Figure 3B. The typical D-band and G-band are observed at approximately $1350\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1585~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, and the intensity of the G-band is greatly enhanced compared to the D-band, indicating a significant increase in the degree of graphitization (Chen et al. 2013; Niu et al. 2017).
The elemental composition and chemical bonding state of $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs are further analyzed using XPS. As shown in Figure 3C, Zn, Cd, S, C can be confirmed in the XPS survey spectra of the sample. Primarily, the high-resolution XPS spectrum of $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ is shown in Figure S2. The two prominent bands at 1022.4 and $1045.8\mathrm{~eV}$ correspond to $\mathrm{Zn~2p}_{3/2}$ and Zn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ peaks of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ (Figure S2A), respectively (Jing et al. 2017; Yang et al. 2014). The Cd 3d spectrum (Figure S2B) shows two distinct bands at 412.1 and $405.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which are easily assigned to the Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ peaks of $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , respectively. Besides, the S 2p spectrum (Figure S2C) confirms the chemical bonding state of $S^{2-}$ at 161.6 and $162.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the S-Cd/S-Zn bonded structure. Additionally, it is noted that the C 1s peak (Figure S2D) at $284.7\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to C-S (Zhou et al. 2015).
The UV-visible DRS is conducted to further evaluate the optical properties of C@ZnCdS MSDCs and ZnCdS DSDCs (Figure 3D). Compared to ZnCdS DSDCs, C@ZnCdS MSDCs exhibits significantly increased visible-light absorption intensity and a broaden absorption range. The distinct differences in optical properties are primarily ascribed to the following two points: (i) the obtained C@ZnCdS MSDCs with triple porous thin shells can sufficiently absorb incident light through the reflection effect between shells; (ii) the introduction of the carbon shell improves electrical conductivity and facilitates electron transport. The results indicate that $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs should be suitable photoactive materials to construct PEC biosensors.
## 3.2. Characterization of TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au composites
The microstructure and morphology of TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au composites are characterized by FESEM (Figure 1F). Additionally, the PXRD analyses of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , C@ZnCdS MSDCs, and TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au composites are shown in Figure 4A. The other details are provided in supporting information.
## 3.3. PEC mechanism
The photo-generated $\mathbf{e}^{-1}/\mathrm{h}^{+}$ transfer mechanism of TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au for the PEC biosensor is showed in Scheme 1D. The bandgap energy $({\mathrm{Eg}})$ of the as-synthesized ZnCdS DSDCs and C@ZnCdS MSDCs could be estimated using the empirical equation:
$$
a h\nu=\mathrm{A}\left(h\nu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{E}\mathbf{g}\right)^{1/2}
$$
Where $a$ , A, and $h\nu$ are the absorption coefficient, a constant, the photon energy, respectively (Kaur and Nagaraja 2017).
The $\mathrm{Eg}$ values of ZnCdS DSDCs (Figure S4) and C@ZnCdS MSDCs (Figure 3E) were calculated (eq 1) to be 2.46 and $2.43\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. This result suggests that triple-shelled porous carbon nanoarchitecture can significantly enhance visible-light harvesting and accelerate electron transfer. The VB potentials of C@ZnCdS MSDCs were measured by VB-XPS (Figure 3F), with band edges of 1.81 eV. The CB potentials of $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs can be calculated by the following empirical formulae (Song et al. 2016):
$$
\mathrm{E_{CB}=E_{V B}-E_{g}}
$$
Where $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{CB}}$ and $\mathrm{{E}_{V B}}$ are the CB and VB edge potentials, respectively.
The $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{CB}}$ potentials of C@ZnCdS MSDCs was calculated $(\mathsf{e q}2)$ to be $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{CB}}{=}-0.62$ eV. The $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{CB}}$ potentials and EVB potentials of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ were -0.5 (NHE) and 2.7 (NHE) eV (Li et al. 2013; Li et al. 2011). As shown in Scheme 1D, C@ZnCdS MSDCs and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ have interlaced band structures, in which both CB and VB positions of $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs are higher than $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Therefore, the synthesized narrow bandgap $({\sim}2.43\ \mathrm{eV})$ C@ZnCdS MSDCs were assembled onto $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ modified ITO electrodes to form a type-II heterostructures. The photoexcited electrons were transferred from the CB of $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs to the CB of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Simultaneously, holes were transferred from the VB of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to the VB of C@ZnCdS MSDCs and then scavenged by an electron donor AA, resulting in enhanced visible-light response and efficient separation of photo-generated $\ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}^{-1}/\ensuremath{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ (Li et al. 2015). In addition, because the Fermi level of Au $(+0.6~\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. NHE) (Misra et al. 2014) is lower than the conduction band edge of $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs (-0.62 V vs. NHE), the decorated Au can act as an effective electron acceptor for C@ZnCdS MSDCs, prohibiting charge recombination (Chiu et al. 2018). Thus, the photocurrent response of the biosensor is enhanced significantly. When anti-CEA and CEA are specifically bound, the electron transfer from AA to TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au composites is hindered, resulting in a decreased photocurrent.
The UV-visible DRS is conducted to further evaluate the optical properties of TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au. In Figure 4B, the absorption wavelength of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is apparently lower than $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ attributed to the wide bandgap $(3.2\mathrm{eV})$ , leading to low efficiency of visible light absorption (Wang et al. 2017). However, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C@\mathrm{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs composite is extended into the visible light region up to $\sim550\mathrm{nm}$ due to the unique morphological features of C@ZnCdS MSDCs and the formation of the type-II heterostructure. On the one hand, the synthesized C@ZnCdS MSDCs exhibit unique morphological advantages of hollow nanostructures (Scheme 1C): (i) the superior light-capturing capability can be mainly ascribed to multiple scattering and reflection in multi-shell porous hollow nanobubble structure (Qian et al. 2009); (ii) triple-shell structure can shorten the transport distance of charge, hence suppressing the charge recombination (Xiao et al. 2018); (iii) the introduction of the carbon shell facilitates electron transport. On the other hand, the type-II TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs heterostructures can significantly enhance the visible-light response and suppresses the recombination of photogenerated $\mathrm{e^{-}/h^{+}}$ . Particularly, the electrodeposition of Au NPs on ITO/TiO2/C $@$ ZnCdS MSDCs shows a broader absorption range and apparently enhanced visible-light absorption intensity, which is attributed to Au NPs as an effective electron acceptor for C@ZnCdS MSDCs, suppressing charge recombination.
