## 3.4. PEC behaviors and EIS
EIS is an effective tool for characterizing interface properties of the electrodes, which can be used to further study the stepwise assembly process of the biosensor (Figure 4C) (Ge et al. 2017). The electron-transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{et})}$ value of the bare ITO electrode exhibited the smallest (curve a), which could be attributed to the fast electrons transfer of the sensing interface. Afterwards, the $\mathrm{\bfR}_{\mathrm{et}}$ constantly increased when $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (curves b), C@ZnCdS MSDCs (curves d) were assembled on the bare ITO surface, which could be attributed to the poor electron transmission capacity of different substances. Nevertheless, after electrodeposition Au NPs on ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs surface (curves c), a smaller $\mathrm{\sfR}_{\mathrm{et}}$ was obtained by increased conductivity. After continuous modification with anti-CEA (curve e), BSA (curve f) and CEA (curve g), the $\mathrm{\bfR}_{\mathrm{et}}$ value increased progressively, which could be due to the hindrance effect and poor conductivity of protein. The results indicate that PEC biosensor was prepared successfully.
To deeply analyze the stepwise construction process of the PEC biosensor, all PEC tests was carried out in PBS $(0.1\ \mathrm{M},\ \mathrm{pH}\ 7.4)$ solution containing $0.2\mathrm{~M~}$ AA (Figure 4D). No photocurrent was observed on the bare ITO electrode (curve a). After modified with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the photocurrent intensity (curve b, $\mathrm{I}{=}6.58~\upmu\mathrm{A},$ of $\mathrm{ITO/TiO}_{2}$ electrode increased slightly. However, the $\mathrm{ITO/TiO}_{2}$ electrode annealed at $450^{\circ}$ for 30 min in air displayed enhancive photocurrent intensity (curve c, $\mathrm{I}{=}22.48~\upmu\mathrm{A}^{\cdot}$ ) which was ascribed to the porous structure of calcined $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Figure S3A). The photocurrent intensity (curve d, $\mathrm{I}{=}190.23~\upmu\mathrm{A},$ ) of ITO/TiO2/C $@$ ZnCdS MSDCs electrode displayed a ${\sim}8.5$ times higher than that of $\mathrm{ITO/TiO}_{2}$ electrode (curve c), ascribed to the truth that complex triple-shelled hollow structures could promote light absorption properties. After electrodeposition Au NPs on ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs electrode surface, the photocurrent intensity (curve e, $\mathrm{I}{=}206.88\quad\upmu\mathrm{A})$ of ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au electrode was increased slightly owing to the enlarged electron-hole separation. When anti-CEA (curve f, $\mathrm{I}{=}143.24\ \upmu\mathrm{A},$ , BSA (curve g, $\mathrm{I=1}29.86~\upmu\mathrm{A})$ and CEA (curve h, $\mathrm{I}{=}84.58~\upmu\mathrm{A}\dot{}$ ) were incubated on
ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au electrode surface successively, the photocurrents decrease gradually, owing to the hindrance effect and poor conductivity of protein.
## 3.5. Stability, reproducibility, and selectivity of the PEC biosensor
The stability of the fabricated PEC biosensor is shown in Figure 4E. The photocurrent response of the biosensor was recorded under ten on/off irradiation cycles for 400 s with $0.1~\mathrm{\ng~mL^{-1}}$ CEA and no significant variation was observed, suggesting the PEC biosensor possesses stable photocurrent response for CEA detection. In addition, the reproductivity of the PEC biosensor was also investigated. For $0.1~\mathrm{{ng}~\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}}}$ CEA, the relative standard deviation (RSD) of $2.5\%$ $92.24\upmu\mathrm{A}$ , 88.23 $\upmu\mathrm{A}$ , $89.63~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ , $87.84~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ , $92.68~\upmu\mathrm{A},$ was obtained for five $\mathrm{ITO/TiO_{2}/C@Z n C d S}$ MSDCs/Au/anti-CEA/BSA/CEA electrodes, indicating the designed biosensor has excellent reproducibility. Furthermore, the selectivity of the PEC biosensor for CEA was studied by testing the photocurrent response of the sensor to several representative interfering substances including prostate specific antigen (PSA), alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), and BSA. No dramatic photocurrent signals change was observed by interfering substances, suggesting that the proposed PEC sensor has satisfactory selectivity (Figure 4F).
## 3.6. PEC assay of CEA
To achieve excellent performance in the PEC detection of CEA, experimental parameters including pH values, immunoreaction time, incubation time, the concentration of C@ZnCdS MSDCs, AA and anti-CEA were optimized (Figure S5). Figure 5A shows that the fabricated PEC biosensor is applied to detect different concentrations of CEA under optimized conditions. The photocurrent response gradually decreases with increasing concentrations of CEA. Figure 5B displays the linear relationship related to the logarithmic values of CEA concentration from $0.00005~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}$ to $500\ \mathrm{ng\mL^{-1}}$ with low LOD of 2.28 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaS/N}=3\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ). The regression equation is $\mathrm{~I~}(\upmu\mathrm{A})=78.7823\mathrm{-}9.4528\mathrm{~log~}C_{\mathrm{CEA}}\mathrm{~(ng~mL^{-1})}$ $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{R}^{2}=0.9981_{.}$ ). The fabricated PEC biosensor for CEA assay showed a relatively wider linear range and lower detection limits than that recently reported (Table S1).
## 3 .7. Application of the biosensor in human serum
To assess the practical applications of the proposed PEC biosensor, different concentrations $(0.10,10.00,100.00\mathrm{ng}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ ) of CEA in human serum samples were detected by standard addition methods. As shown in Table S2, the RSD was $1.73{-}3.79\%$ and recoveries were $97.3\substack{-105.7\%}$ , indicating the practicability of designed PEC biosensor for CEA detection in serum samples.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, C@ZnCdS MSDCs with triple porous thin shells were successfully synthesized for the first time and used as a photoactive material for PEC biosensor. Benefiting from its unique morphological characteristics, C@ZnCdS MSDCs exhibited significantly enhanced visible-light harvesting. The construction of TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs heterostructure can effectively suppresses the recombination of photo-generated $\mathrm{e^{-1}/h^{+}}$ . Moreover, after Au NPs deposited on the surface of ITO/TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs, the absorption capacity of visible light was further enhanced. Meanwhile, the PEC mechanism of TiO2/C@ZnCdS MSDCs/Au system was initially studied. The proposed PEC biosensor exhibited a low detection limit and wide linear range, excellent selectivity, stability, and reproducibility in CEA analysis.
In this paper, the application of $\mathrm{C@{ZnCdS}}$ MSDCs as photoactive material in PEC biosensor was initially explored. Further work will center on the optimization of experimental conditions and the construction of novel heterojunctions.
